page("tailgate-grilling-guide","Tailgate Grilling Guide: Portable Charcoal Grill in the Parking Lot","How to tailgate with a portable charcoal grill: venue rules, where to set up, a timeline, a crowd-friendly menu and ash handling.","full13",f"""
<p>Tailgating is a timed event: you have a few hours, a parking space and a crowd. A grill that comes out of the trunk flat and sets up in about a minute (Slot Grill says 30 to 60 seconds once you've done it a couple of times) buys you time for the fun part.</p>
<h2>Before game day</h2>{ul(["Read the venue's tailgate rules. Many allow charcoal with conditions; some ban open flames or require ash bins.","Prep at home: patties formed, sausages ready, vegetables cut, everything labeled in the cooler.","Pack a lidded metal can for ash and water for emergencies."])}
<h2>Setting up</h2>{ul(["On pavement, behind or beside the vehicle with clearance, away from foot traffic and fuel.","Never on the tailgate itself, in a truck bed, or on a plastic folding table.","Chairs and kids on the other side of the table from the fire."])}
<h2>Timeline</h2>{table(["Before kickoff","Do"],[["3 hrs","Park, set up, light a chimney"],["2.5 hrs","Sausages and wings"],["1.5 hrs","Burgers or smash burgers"],["1 hr","Stop adding coals; dessert on the last heat"],["Leave","Fire out or ash in the metal can per venue rules"]])}
<h2>Which size</h2><p>The <a href="{COMPACT}">Compact</a> (16.25&quot; × 9&quot; cooking area) suits a few friends. For a bigger crowd, the <a href="{FULL}">Full Size</a> (20.5&quot; × 11.25&quot;) or both. See {L("compact-vs-full-size-slot-grill","Compact vs Full Size")}.</p>
""","places",[("Can I grill in a stadium parking lot?","Many venues allow it with rules, some don't. Check the venue's tailgating policy before you go.")])

page("campsite-grilling-guide","Campsite Grilling Guide","Cooking at a campsite with a portable charcoal grill: campground rules, picking a spot, a three-day menu, and leaving the site clean.","c28",f"""
<p>Campground fire rings are often too big, too hot or too dirty for real cooking. A portable grill gives you controlled heat right where you want it.</p>
<h2>Rules</h2>{ul(["Check campground fire rules and any burn ban at check-in.","Use the site's fire ring, grill pad or bare ground, away from tents, trees and vehicles.","Never cook inside a tent, vestibule or camper. Charcoal produces carbon monoxide."])}
<h2>A three-day menu</h2>{table(["","Breakfast","Dinner"],[["Day 1","(drive in)","{0}".format(L("foil-packet-potatoes-and-sausage","Foil-packet sausage and potatoes"))],["Day 2","{0}".format(L("griddle-pancakes-and-bacon","Pancakes and bacon")),"{0}".format(L("teriyaki-chicken-skewers","Teriyaki chicken skewers"))],["Day 3","Eggs and toast on the griddle","Burgers, then pack up"]])}
<h2>After dinner</h2><p>Slot Grill's FAQ says the grill can serve as a fire pit after supper where campfires are allowed. Keep it small and never unattended.</p>
<h2>Leave it clean</h2><p>Drown and stir ash until cold or let it burn out, then dispose of it where the campground directs. Pack out all trash.</p>
""","places",[])

page("rv-grilling-guide","RV Grilling Guide","Using a portable charcoal grill on RV trips: park rules, where to set up on the pad, storage in the rig, and keeping soot out.","c07",f"""
<p>RV storage is precious. A grill that packs flat (about 1&quot; for the Compact, about 1.25&quot; for the Full Size) slides into a basement bay or behind a seat.</p>
<h2>At the RV park</h2>{ul(["Check park rules; some allow charcoal only in provided pits or on the pad.","Set up on the pad or gravel, well away from the rig, awning, slide-outs and propane.","Never under an awning or inside."])}
<h2>Storage</h2>{ul(["Only pack it cold and empty.","Bag it to keep soot off the bay.","Keep charcoal in a sealed bin; it absorbs moisture and odors."])}
""","places",[])

page("beach-grilling-guide","Beach Grilling Guide","Grilling on the beach with a portable charcoal grill: local beach rules, wind, sand, food safety in the sun and ash disposal.","c07",f"""
<h2>Rules vary a lot</h2><p>Some beaches allow charcoal grills, some only in designated areas, some ban fires. Check the city, county or park rules before you go.</p>
<h2>Setting up on sand</h2>{ul(["Pick firm, flat sand away from dunes, grass and umbrellas.","Wiggle the grill into the sand so it sits level.","Coal end into the wind; bring extra charcoal."])}
<h2>Food in the sun</h2><p>Keep the cooler shaded and the lid closed. Don't leave food out more than 1 hour above 90°F.</p>
<h2>Never bury coals</h2><p>Buried coals stay hot for hours and burn bare feet. Drown and stir, pack ash out in a metal can or use designated bins.</p>
<h2>After</h2><p>Rinse off sand and salt, dry, and oil the carbon steel. See {L("cleaning-a-portable-grill-on-the-road","cleaning on the road")}.</p>
""","places",[])

page("park-and-picnic-grilling","Park and Picnic Area Grilling","Bringing a portable charcoal grill to a park or picnic area: rules, setup on a pad, a picnic menu and cleanup.","c13",f"""
<p>Many parks have charcoal rules: provided grills only, designated areas, or portable grills allowed on pads. Check before you go.</p>
<h2>Setup</h2>{ul(["On concrete, gravel or bare dirt, never on a wooden picnic table.","Out of the path of kids and dogs.","Water nearby."])}
<h2>Picnic menu</h2>{ul(["{0}".format(L("picnic-burgers-on-a-portable-grill","Picnic burgers")),"{0}".format(L("griddle-grilled-cheese-and-quesadillas","Grilled cheese or quesadillas")),"Fruit for dessert"])}
<h2>Cleanup</h2><p>Use park hot-coal bins if provided; otherwise let ash cool and pack it out.</p>
""","places",[])

page("backyard-patio-grilling","Backyard and Small Patio Grilling","Using a portable flat-pack charcoal grill at home: small patios, safe surfaces, clearance, and storing it indoors between cooks.","c08",f"""
<p>A portable grill isn't just for trips. If your patio is small, a grill that stores flat in a closet keeps the space usable.</p>
<h2>Check the rules</h2><p>Apartments, condos, HOAs and local fire codes may restrict charcoal on balconies and near buildings. Check first.</p>
<h2>Safe setup</h2>{ul(["Concrete, pavers or gravel. Not a wood or composite deck, plastic tiles, artificial turf or grass.","Clearance from walls, siding, railings, overhangs and furniture.","The Slot Grill's air gap helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below, but it doesn't make a combustible surface safe."])}
<h2>Storage</h2><p>Store it indoors, flat and dry. Carbon steel left out in the rain rusts.</p>
""","places",[])

page("fishing-and-boat-day-shore-cooking","Fishing and Boat Day Shore Cooking","Taking a portable charcoal grill on a fishing or boat day: carrying it as cargo and cooking only on shore.","c13",f"""
<p>A flat-pack grill stows easily aboard. Slot Grill's FAQ is clear about the rule: carry it as cargo, cook on shore (a sandbar or beach) on a stable non-combustible surface where fires are permitted, and never light a charcoal grill on a boat deck or near the boat.</p>
<h2>Shore setup</h2>{ul(["Sand or gravel above the waterline.","Away from the boat, fuel and vegetation.","Bucket of water for ash."])}
<h2>Cooking your catch</h2><p>Follow local regulations. Keep fish on ice until cooking; cook to 145°F or until opaque and flaky.</p>
""","places",[])

page("hunting-and-work-site-lunch","Hunting Camp and Job Site Lunch on a Portable Grill","Using a portable charcoal grill for a hunting camp or a job-site lunch: fast menus, setup and cleanup.","full08",f"""
<p>A heavy steel grill in the truck means hot lunch anywhere you're allowed to have a fire.</p>
<h2>Fast menus</h2>{ul(["Sausage on bread","Smash burgers on a griddle plate","Leftover reheats in a skillet (to 165°F)"])}
<h2>Rules</h2>{ul(["Ask the landowner or site manager.","Bare ground or gravel, clear of equipment and fuel.","Out cold before you leave."])}
""","places",[])
